# Round 58: pins the first-use SLIDE. `w is f` is a pure-data mention -- it
# cannot invoke anything -- so f's first-use slides forward to the next
# call-bearing statement (the print), which the binding `v is 10` provably
# precedes. The pre-slide rule counted the mention itself as the first use
# and refused this VM-valid program (the module-scope function-table idiom:
# table built above the data globals its handlers write). The alias-call-
# before-bind shape stays refused: invocation through any alias still
# requires executing a call-bearing statement, and the slide lands on it.
define f() as:
    v is v + 1
    return v
w is f
v is 10
print of (w of [])
